{
  "text": "Your screen isn't playing a video. It's flipping still pictures. 60, 120, even 144 every second. Two numbers decide how smooth it feels. Frames per second: how fast your chip draws pictures. Hertz: how often your screen refreshes. At 60, each picture gets 16.7 milliseconds. At 120, just 8.3. At 144, under seven. Every refresh, the screen redraws itself row by row. Inside each pixel, tiny transistors hold its color until the next refresh. This video only plays at 60, so let's slow time down. At 60 hertz, a moving ball jumps in big steps. At 120, the steps are half as big, so motion looks smoother. But fast refresh drains battery, so phones cheat. LTPO screens shift speed on the fly: 120 when you scroll, slower when you read, 1 hertz on the lock screen. Gaming phones push to 144 or 165 hertz, with touch sensors checking your finger thousands of times a second. On a PC, the graphics card must keep up. Say it makes 90 frames for a 144 hertz monitor. The screen catches half of one frame and half of the next. That's tearing. The fix is variable refresh, like G-Sync or FreeSync. The monitor waits for each new frame, and refreshes the moment it lands. And each jump gives less. 60 to 120 saves 8 milliseconds a frame. 120 to 144, under one and a half. Yet esports monitors now hit 600. So, frames per second is how fast pictures are made. Hertz is how fast they're shown. Smooth is when they match. Follow for part two: what input lag really is.",
